body:
### Brief summary

As reported by a user on the [OSS support forum](https://community.k6.io/t/bug-with-k6-threshold-validation/5038), there seems to be an issue with Thresholds applying to the median value of Trend metrics. Both core and custom metrics are affected.

Setting a threshold on a Trend metric's value, such as `http_req_duration`, will always fail, regardless of the expression used.

### k6 version

>= v0.40.0

### OS

Docker + Linux

### Docker version and image (if applicable)

loadimpact/k6:latest

### Steps to reproduce the problem

Here is an example script demonstrating the issue. It depends on having [Lhotse](https://github.com/oleiade/lhotse) running locally to simulate the desired performance of a target HTTP server. 

```javascript
import { check } from "k6";
import http from "k6/http";
import { Trend } from "k6/metrics";

export let options = {
  thresholds: {
    http_req_duration: ["med>39"],
    custom_trend: ["med>39"],
  },

  scenarios: {
    issueHappensWithCoreTrendMetric: {
      executor: "constant-arrival-rate",
      exec: "exampleFunction",
      rate: 1,
      timeUnit: "30s",
      duration: "30s",
      preAllocatedVUs: 2,
      exec: "issueHappensWithCoreTrendMetric",
    },
    issueHappensWithCustomTrendMetric: {
      executor: "constant-arrival-rate",
      exec: "exampleFunction",
      rate: 1,
      timeUnit: "30s",
      duration: "30s",
      preAllocatedVUs: 2,
      exec: "issueHappensWithCustomTrendMetric",
    },
  },
};

export function issueHappensWithCoreTrendMetric() {
  // Server produces requests that will take between 70, and 80ms
  // to complete. Hence, the resulting http_req_duration metric
  // should have a median value well above the 39ms threshold.
  const res = http.get("http://localhost:3434/latency/70ms-80ms", {}, {});

  check(res, {
    "is status 200": (r) => r.status === 200,
  });
}

const customTrend = new Trend("custom_trend");

export function issueHappensWithCustomTrendMetric() {
  // Add values to the custom trend metric so that its median
  // value is well above the 39ms threshold.
  customTrend.add(70);
  customTrend.add(80);
  customTrend.add(90);
}
```

In this example, both thresholds on the `http_req_duration`, and `custom_trend` will fail, although the performance of the target HTTP server, and the values fed into the Trend should lead them to pass.

### Expected behaviour

We would expect thresholds to pass.

### Actual behaviour

```
     checks.........................: 100.00% ✓ 1                    ✗ 0  
   ✗ custom_trend...................: avg=80       min=70       med=80       max=90       p(90)=90       p(95)=90      
     data_received..................: 156 B   5.1996295251830835 B/s
     data_sent......................: 97 B    3.233102973992046 B/s
     http_req_blocked...............: avg=271.89µs min=271.89µs med=271.89µs max=271.89µs p(90)=271.89µs p(95)=271.89µs
     http_req_connecting............: avg=148.23µs min=148.23µs med=148.23µs max=148.23µs p(90)=148.23µs p(95)=148.23µs
   ✗ http_req_duration..............: avg=75.52ms  min=75.52ms  med=75.52ms  max=75.52ms  p(90)=75.52ms  p(95)=75.52ms 
       { expected_response:true }...: avg=75.52ms  min=75.52ms  med=75.52ms  max=75.52ms  p(90)=75.52ms  p(95)=75.52ms 
     http_req_failed................: 0.00%   ✓ 0                    ✗ 1  
     http_req_receiving.............: avg=86.24µs  min=86.24µs  med=86.24µs  max=86.24µs  p(90)=86.24µs  p(95)=86.24µs 
     http_req_sending...............: avg=73.97µs  min=73.97µs  med=73.97µs  max=73.97µs  p(90)=73.97µs  p(95)=73.97µs 
     http_req_tls_handshaking.......: avg=0s       min=0s       med=0s       max=0s       p(90)=0s       p(95)=0s      
     http_req_waiting...............: avg=75.36ms  min=75.36ms  med=75.36ms  max=75.36ms  p(90)=75.36ms  p(95)=75.36ms 
     http_reqs......................: 1       0.033331/s
     iteration_duration.............: avg=25.42ms  min=65.8µs   med=68.75µs  max=76.12ms  p(90)=60.91ms  p(95)=68.51ms 
     iterations.....................: 3       0.099993/s
     vus............................: 4       min=4                  max=4
     vus_max........................: 4       min=4                  max=4
```

Although the displayed median values are consistent with a threshold that should be passing, we observe the opposite.
